Ix.Kernel notices
=================

IxCK/Kernel is Ix's certified kernel. Most of it is derived from con-leche,
the verified Lean kernel checker at https://github.com/leanprover/con-leche,
received under the Apache License, Version 2.0; con-leche's licence is
LICENSE-CON-LECHE in this directory. Upstream holds the copyright of the
con-leche files; the changes are Argument Computer Corporation's, under the
same licence. The code is maintained in this repository and is expected to
diverge from upstream.

Origin
------

The files of con-leche's ConLeche/ tree that the checker, the Ixon reader
and the main theorem use, at revision
ae0c0c4e4ce6a0081648aff03fe9c39d002c4526, except seven taken at revision
3ca9e2fe749a51cba4c6e3527aeecba074c29316 (upstream task #323):
Cached/CoreC.lean, Core.lean, Verify/BetaSpine.lean,
Verify/Cached/DiscC4.lean, Verify/InferLeaves.lean, Verify/InferLemmas.lean
and Verify/Rules/RedBridge.lean.

Outside this directory, at the first revision: Tests/Ix/Kernel/Axioms.lean
is derived from con-leche's tests/ConLecheTests/Axioms.lean and modified;
Tests/Ix/Kernel/Layering.lean and Tests/Ix/Kernel/TrustSurface.lean are
derived from con-leche's tests/layering.sh and tests/trust-surface.sh
(Apache-2.0) and modified; Tests/Fixtures/trust-surface/lexer.lean is
con-leche's tests/trust-surface/lexer.lean, unchanged.

Modifications
-------------

  * Every derived file was moved from ConLeche/Kernel/X or ConLeche/X to
    IxCK/Kernel/X, and the namespace and module prefix ConLeche (and
    ConLeche.Kernel) was renamed to Ix.Kernel.
  * Primitives by specification (2026-10): the checker validates the
    constants an input role table binds against fixed specifications
    (Roles.lean, this repository's) instead of pin tables. CheckerBase.lean
    imports Roles instead of upstream's NatOpPins, which splices JSON pin
    dumps at elaboration time (NatOpPins.lean is not included).
    Checker.lean: the operations' install gate checks the table's evidence
    (checkDivModEvidence, under the closure condition evidenceLocal;
    divModCertsGuard), the reduce opaques keep their identity certificate
    (checkReduceIdentity), and the Nat.div/Nat.mod pin variants,
    checkDivModPinAt and checkReducePin are gone; TrustAxioms.lean drops
    the reduce pin; DeclCheck.lean and Cached/ParsedC.lean mirror the gate.
    The proofs that consume the gate (Cached/Installed.lean,
    Model/{Fold,Harvest,DivModCert,ReduceOps,Annot/Laws}.lean,
    Semantics/{DeclRun,Bridge/*}.lean, Verify/**) take the evidence where
    they took the pin list, and their statements hold for every evidence.
    TrustPins.lean is removed.
  * Level.lean: when both branches of the level comparison's (param, max)
    case fail, the comparison falls back on Géran's sublevels
    (LevelGeran.lean), which decide the case nanoda's branch split misses.
    Verify/Level.lean extends the soundness proof to that answer.
  * Frontend/InModel/Nested.lean forms a nested block's container groups
    largest family first, and declines a group that would share a member
    with an earlier one, so that the result does not depend on the order
    of the block's auxiliary motives.
  * Levels the ∀ rule shares (2026-11): Expr.lean gives Level a second
    computed field, its has-parameter bit (hasParamData), and levelHasParam
    runs as that field (a proved @[csimp]); Cached/ExprNodes.lean's census
    names it. The ∀ rule keeps the original's level, imax u v (an earlier
    change that folded it, Level.mkIMax, was withdrawn); Cached/StateC.lean
    adds a table to the cached core's state and internLM, which returns
    its argument (a hit only after == confirms it), and Cached/CoreC.lean
    hash-conses the rule's level with it at its two sites (inferPisOutI,
    inferBodyIOI); Verify/Cached/SimCEff.lean proves internLM_eff and
    Verify/Cached/{BinderLoopC,DiscC4}.lean use it. With the plain imax, a
    ∀ chain over one shared type builds a level whose tree doubles per
    binder, the level functions walk levels as trees, and the binder loop
    rebuilds equal levels as new objects. Expr.lean's Level.beqPtr (the executed Level.beq,
    by a proved @[csimp]) decides its structural case by a descent
    memoised on the pairs it proves equal (Level.decMemo, Expr.beqMemo's
    arrangement), so that equal levels built apart as DAGs, which the
    folds compare, are compared in time linear in their DAGs.
  * Level walks on shared levels (2026-11): Level.lean runs simplify,
    leqCore, zeronessOf and isNeverZero, through four proved @[csimp]
    theorems (simplify_eq_simplifyFast, leqCore_eq_leqCoreFast,
    zeronessOf_eq_zeronessOfFast, isNeverZero_eq_isNeverZeroFast), as
    walks memoised per call on the nodes they finish (for leqCore, on its
    subproblems (fuel, l, r, diff), with a memoised subst, substMemo, in
    its case split), and below a small tree size as exact copies of the
    plain recursion. The functions themselves are unchanged. Levels that
    the ∀ rule builds, and that unfolding builds by instantiating at
    max u u, are shared as DAGs whose trees are exponentially larger, and
    leqCore's distributions meet the same subproblems again.
  * Native stack per step and per record (2026-11): Cached/CoreC.lean runs
    whnfCoreLoopI, whnfLoopI and defeqLoopI, through three proved @[csimp]
    theorems (whnfCoreLoopI_eq_whnfCoreLoopFast, whnfLoopI_eq_whnfLoopFast,
    defeqLoopI_eq_defeqLoopFast), as loops over a twin of the step that
    returns its tail call of the continuation instead of making it
    (whnfCoreStepR, whnfAppR, betaPeelR, whnfStepR, defeqStepR; each step
    proved to factor through its twin); FEnv.lean runs mkFEnvGo
    (mkFEnvGo_eq_mkFEnvGoFast) as a fold over the reversed list and
    Units.lean runs unitsGo (unitsGo_eq_unitsGoFast) with an accumulator.
    The functions themselves are unchanged. The loops called their
    continuation through a closure, which kept native frames per
    reduction step, and the two recursions kept one per installed
    constant or declaration.
  * Telescopes that stop at a checked tail (2026-11): Cached/StateC.lean
    adds peelFuelTo and peelFuelToM, and Cached/CoreC.lean's inference and
    annotation clauses for ∀ and λ take the binder loops' peel fuel from
    them (the binders before the first closed residual the memo already
    holds) in place of the constant peelFuel; the loops' simulations hold
    for every peel fuel, and Verify/Cached/SimCEff.lean proves
    peelFuelToM_eff, which Verify/Cached/{DiscC4,DiscC6}.lean take in place
    of peelFuelM_eff. StateC.lean's isEquivLM simplifies through
    simplifyMemoC, Level.simplifyGo seeded with the declaration's memo
    (simplifyMemoC_spec). A chain T (j+1) = T j → T j re-peeled the whole
    telescope below each step, and annotation rebuilt it as fresh nodes.
  * A 64-bit term hash (2026-11): Expr.lean computes the node hash as a
    field of its own (Expr.hashW, the original mixHash recurrence without
    truncation) instead of the low 32 bits kept in the packed word, whose
    upper bits are now reserved; Expr.beqMemo tests both words before
    descending. A chain of nodes built by one step made the 32-bit hashes
    iterate one function and cycle, and memo probes then descended terms
    of equal hash.
  * Phase B's task bound (2026-11): Cached/Installed.lean's
    checkPendingTasks takes a bound on its tasks, and checkDecls,
    checkDeclsUnder and checkDeclsWithUserAxioms (Cached/Policy.lean) take
    a defaulted FoldConfig carrying it; checkPendingTasks_eq holds for
    every bound and checkDecls_cfg, checkDeclsUnder_cfg show the setting
    changes no result. One task per 64 records let the number of threads,
    each reserving a stack, grow with the input.
  * MainTheorem.lean keeps only model_exists; the corollary about NDJSON
    files and the imports it needs are dropped.
  * Verify/Cached/AgreeFloor.lean and Verify/Cached/PushChain.lean import
    Init.LetFun with `import all`: Lean 4.34.0 no longer exposes the body
    of letFun, which these proofs unfold.
  * Tests/Ix/Kernel/Axioms.lean keeps the axiom guards on the modules Ix
    uses, under the renamed namespace.

